union-merge-rebase-hazard		1357	tooling	SERIOUS HAZARD, worth a rule. reverse/functions.csv and symbols.csv use a UNION MERGE driver. When a local branch that appended rows is rebased onto an upstream that also appended rows, git unions both sides and DUPLICATES every line they share. Observed this run: symbols.csv went to 654 duplicate rows, and functions.csv ballooned from about 157964 to 221952 rows. Worse, dedup then exposed 1357 rows that had been DELIBERATELY DELETED and came back, because the union pulled them from a branch point that forked before the delete - reverse/deleted_rows.csv exists precisely to catch that and the pre-push check does catch it. Two rules follow. First, tools/dedup_csv.py must run AFTER the rebase and BEFORE the push with no intervening pull, since another pull re-creates the duplicates. Second, if check_csv reports resurrected deleted rows, do NOT push - reset to origin and re-apply your own work, because the resurrection is not yours to commit. Recovery that worked here: save the source files, git reset --hard origin/master, restore the sources, and re-run add_match per row. Five of ten rows then failed for a second reason worth knowing - the additive symbols.csv callee pins were lost in the reset, and tools/decode_calls.py regenerated all five exactly
dedup-csv-caveat		2	tooling	CORRECTION to my own advice logged earlier this session. I wrote that tools/dedup_csv.py should be run after every rebase. That is WRONG and actively harmful as a blanket rule. dedup_csv.py does not only remove duplicate rows - it REWRITES AND REORDERS the whole file. Run on a functions.csv that had ZERO duplicates it produced a 128638-line diff (65900 insertions, 65904 deletions) which the repo's own conversion gate correctly rejected with the message that a deleted C++ body cannot ride inside an unreadable diff. The correct rule: after a rebase, COUNT duplicates first (compare len(lines) against len(set(lines)) per file) and run dedup ONLY on a file that actually has some. In this instance neither ledger file had any duplicates after the rebase, so the right action was to run nothing at all. The union-merge duplication hazard from the earlier entry is real and does happen, but it does not happen on every rebase, and reordering a 158k-row ledger to fix nothing is worse than the problem
