selection-signal-zero-call		1	tooling	SELECTION SIGNAL, the first one this project has that actually predicts landability. A candidate whose body contains ZERO external CALL and JMP instructions - pure register and stack loads plus straight-line field stores through this+offset - lands clean, typically on the first compile. NetGameCommandMsg's constructor at 0x00673640 (57 bytes) matched exactly on the first attempt on exactly this basis. The reason is mechanical rather than statistical: no external call means no unresolved-callee displacement to get wrong, and no shared subroutine that MSVC could inline into or out of, so the two hazards that account for most near-misses are both structurally absent. Every other sub-80-byte candidate hand-disassembled in the same pass FAILED this test and none of them landed - they either called an RVA absent from functions.csv entirely (ObjectPool::Free_Object_Memory, CrateCollide ctors, Object::getGroup, UpgradeCenterFindUpgradeShim), tail-jumped through a second unresolved thunk layer (NameKeyObjectCreationPairDestructorShim::destroy), or belonged to a class whose header shows field-offset drift against the bytes actually compiling. Important correction to the standing advice: SIZE ALONE IS A WEAKER FILTER THAN CALL COUNT. Several 60-80 byte rows were unsafe and the 57-byte win worked because of its SHAPE not its size. Prefer call-count zero over small size when ordering the pool. Also worth noting ThreadClass::Set_Priority and its ctor are both off by a consistent +8 against compiled bytes - a real header bug, recorded but deliberately not fixed blind mid-lane
