ini-preprocessmacro-deadstore	0x008524B0	148	attempted	Reverse-engineered in full and reached 130 of 148 bytes correct - all branch structure, the register choice for the begin/end loads, and the hash loop matched exactly - before hitting a residual. INI::preprocessMacro is a hash-table macro lookup: digits 0-9 skip the lookup and return the token unchanged, non-digits compute a rolling XOR/shift hash over the string, index a bucket array through begin/end globals which are read as plain pointer VALUES rather than as arrays, walk a singly-linked MacroNode{next,hash,value} chain, and return value+8 (an AsciiString-style buffer) or g_NAMEKEY_empty_string as fallback, else the original token. Two things worth keeping. First, the header comment describing a '-' check is WRONG - '-' is not special-cased, it falls through the c<'0' branch into the hash path like any other non-digit, so that comment describes dead code. Second, the remaining 18-byte gap is retail allocating an 8-byte stack frame and storing a dead default-text value into a stack slot that is never read back on any live path: a dead-store scheduling residual of the same family as the documented EH-frame and vptr-store scheduling residuals. Restructuring with volatile, with for versus while, and with separate begin/end locals all failed to reproduce it. Reverted cleanly and verified byte-identical to HEAD by checksum. Treat dead-store scheduling as a named member of the residual list from now on
