pins-kept-as-independent-facts	0x0077F760	7	converted	One row landed from the callee-shape sweep - buildFieldParse@BaikonurLaunchPowerModuleData at 0x0077F760 (30 bytes) - by pinning its base-class forward call through ILT thunk 0x00022584 to the already-matched body at 0x0077D380. Seven pins were appended to symbols.csv in total and SIX of them belong to candidates that did NOT land: ensureUniqueBufferOfSize@AsciiString (thunk 0x00032182, body 0x0014F280), operator= on StreakRendererClass (0x0095C860), drawCheckBoxText (0x0078FDE0), markAsDead@AIUpdateInterface (thunk 0x00025BFD, body 0x0026EE80), deselectObject@GameLogic (thunk 0x0001EC2C, body 0x00382F50), and the LANGameSlot destructor (thunk 0x00029A69, body 0x006858A0). Each was verified by decode_calls against a byte-true call displacement, so each is an independently true fact about the binary regardless of whether its host candidate ever lands - and since new pins are exactly what convert a no-name blocker into a landable row, keeping them is the point. Note for a future pass: several name a gen-dump body at a DIFFERENT address from the thunk, which makes those bodies claimable candidates in their own right. Separate lead worth following: FontDesc's constructor target makes an extra two-argument AsciiString::set(ptr,16) call absent from our body, suggesting BFME's FontDesc carries a field or construction step the ZH reference header does not
ledger-absent-category-overstated	0x0091CD30	4	identified	CORRECTION to the claim made in commit 74b33dbcc. The four engine-ref accessors were described there as revealing a large structurally-invisible category of functions absent from functions.csv. That was overstated. The category is real BY NAME COUNT - 339 files across GameEngine and GameEngineDevice carry present-unmatched markers, thousands of unclaimed definitions, and roughly 20-30 genuinely ledger-absent candidates were confirmed in WW3D2 alone - but almost none of it is PLACEABLE, which is the part that matters. Root cause, found by instrumenting a scratch copy of the tool: tools/locate.py line 33 sets MIN_NEEDLE = 8, so its masked byte-scan refuses to attempt placement unless the function has at least 8 consecutive RELOCATION-FREE bytes. Every trivial one-line accessor - return a member, bump a counter, a single float or int getter - compiles to between 2 and 7 relocation-free bytes and is discarded as WEAK NEEDLE before any uniqueness check runs. Anything larger in these files came back honestly unlocated, meaning drifted or absent from this binary. So the pool splits into things too SMALL for the scanner to place and things too DRIFTED to find, with very little in between. Sampled across roughly 60 present-unmatched functions in 9 GameEngine files and 8 WW3D2 files with zero landings. Do not schedule this as a general lane on the strength of the four accessors
adjacency-placement-is-the-method	0x0091CD30	4	tooling	THE GENERALISABLE METHOD hiding behind the four engine-ref accessors, and the reason they landed when the byte-scan approach cannot work for bodies of their size. They were not placed by a byte scan at all - at 4 bytes they fall far below locate.py's 8-byte needle floor. They were placeable because of ADJACENCY: each sat in an address gap bracketed on both sides by ALREADY-MATCHED rows, 16-byte aligned, leaving exactly one consistent assignment. That is a placement signal completely independent of byte entropy, and it is the only one that can work for sub-8-byte bodies. The WW3D2 neighbourhood was checked exhaustively for further instances of the pattern and has NONE left - dx8vertexbuffer.cpp and dx8indexbuffer.cpp are confirmed fully harvested and no sibling file has an equivalent gap - which is why that lane returned nothing. But the check was only ever run against a handful of files. The obvious next step is a tool that enumerates, binary-wide, every address gap between consecutive matched rows and cross-references the gap size and alignment against ledger-absent definitions of a compatible compiled size. That would make the sub-8-byte population reachable for the first time. Worth building before running another pass at this category
pinfollow-six-bodies-blocked	0x0014F280	6	identified	All six bodies named by the pins added in 74b33dbcc were investigated and every one hit a structural blocker rather than an idiom difference, so none were claimed and nothing was written. One is a REFUTATION worth recording: the body at 0x0014F280, reached through ILT thunk 0x00032182 and pinned as AsciiString::ensureUniqueBufferOfSize, diverges from BYTE ZERO with an entirely different instruction stream including an SEH prologue our body never emits. A pin decoded from a byte-true call displacement gives a trustworthy ADDRESS, but that does not by itself establish that the body sitting at the far end is the function the name says - treat thunk-to-body hops as a second inference needing its own evidence. The other five produced real LAYOUT LEADS rather than rows, and these are the run's usable output. StreakRendererClass::operator= at 0x0095C860 copies dword fields through +0x40 where our compiled body stops at +0x2C, and uses inc word ptr [eax+4] plus a call to a shared release helper where ours inlines a dword increment - BFME's StreakRendererClass carries more members than the ZH-derived header. drawCheckBoxText at 0x0078FDE0 matches for about 20 bytes then makes an extra vtable call through [edx+0x38] with several slot offsets shifted (+0xC versus +0x10, +0x3C versus +0x34), consistent with a GameWindow vtable carrying extra virtual slots in BFME. AIUpdateInterface::markAsDead at 0x0026EE80 is very close - same size, same call graph - but reads m_object at [esi+0xC] where our header puts it at [esi+8], indicating one extra 4-byte field ahead of it. Each of those three is a header fix that would unblock siblings, which is worth more than a single row
vertexmaterial-getopacity-alias-collision	0x00921130	1	identified	Small but reusable trap found while sweeping WW3D2. VertexMaterialClass::Get_Opacity has a symbols.csv entry at 0x00921130, but that address is ALREADY claimed in functions.csv under a different mangled name through a gen-alias row, dup_00921130 aliasing a generated nested-offset float getter. This is the trivial-accessor collision case: any two one-line getters returning a float from the same structure offset compile to byte-identical code, the linker folds them, and whichever name got claimed first owns the address. A symbols.csv pin existing for a name is therefore NOT evidence that the address is available - always check functions.csv for an existing claim at that RVA, including gen-alias and dup_ rows, before treating a pinned accessor as a candidate. Also confirmed while there: TextureBaseClass's eight _Get_Total_* static counters (loop bodies of 116-145 bytes) are genuinely absent from both ledgers but locate.py reports every one unlocated, so they are drifted or absent from this binary rather than merely unclaimed
adjacency-lane-works-quantified	0x007AE640	1627	tooling	LANE CONFIRMED and now tooled, after the byte-scan route was shown to be structurally incapable of reaching this population. tools/find_gaps.py is installed in the repo. It streams functions.csv for matched rows only, sorts by RVA, sweeps for inter-row gaps with a two-pointer pass against ghidra_functions.csv and reloc_names.csv boundary lists, reads the retail image directly to count non-0xCC bytes so pure alignment padding is discarded, and drops gaps whose only known boundary is an SEH Unwind@ data marker. Verified figures reproduced from a clean run: 70275 raw non-empty gaps binary-wide, 3995 remaining after padding and stub-neighbour filtering (use --exclude-stub-neighbors --min-real-bytes 1), and 1627 of those are 32 bytes or under holding at most one known boundary - the unambiguous single-small-function tier. Cross-referencing those against per-file present-unmatched marker counts, requiring the same source file on BOTH flanking matched rows, narrows to 78 gaps with a plausible ledger-absent home. That 78 is a SAME-TU HEURISTIC ONLY, not a name match, and it cannot be made into one cheaply because MSVC's placement order for out-of-line virtuals does not follow source order - each still needs a manual byte read plus mangled-name reconstruction. So this is a standing lane worth a few rows per session, not a batch win. PROOF IT REACHES WHAT NOTHING ELSE COULD: the two rows landed here, ActiveBody::getFrontCrushed at 0x007AE640 and getBackCrushed at 0x007AE660, are SEVEN BYTES each - below locate.py's MIN_NEEDLE of 8, hence permanently invisible to the masked byte-scan. Both were mov al,[ecx+0x84]/[ecx+0x85] then ret, matching inline virtual getters the vendored ActiveBody.h already declares correctly; no source edit was needed at all, because the out-of-line body is emitted only to have an address for the vtable. ActiveBody.cpp went to 27/27. REUSABLE CORROBORATION TRICK worth copying: before landing, each