aiupdate-drift-not-uniform-and-lead-was-backwards	0x0026EE80	57	converted	markAsDead@AIUpdateInterface landed at 0x0026EE80 (57 bytes) via an isolated sibling thunk with a local minimal replica padded to the true offsets, following the setQueueForPathTime and privateGoProne precedents; the shared vendored header was not touched. But the LEAD THAT MOTIVATED IT WAS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTION AND SHAPE, and the correction matters more than the row. The lead recorded retail reading m_object at [esi+0xC] against our [esi+8], implying one extra field. Direct measurement says the OPPOSITE: retail has m_object at +0x08 where the header-compiled layout puts it at +0x0C, so retail is 4 bytes SMALLER there. And the drift is NOT a single uniform shift of any sign - the m_isAiDead / m_isInUpdate flag pair sits at retail +0x32B and +0x330 against header-compiled +0x211 and +0x216, which is retail LARGER by 0x11A, with the two flags block-shifted together since they stay 5 bytes apart in both layouts. Two independently measured, differently-signed deltas in one class. This corroborates rather than contradicts existing in-repo findings in AIUpdateInterface_setQueueForPathTime_Thunk.cpp and the friend_notifyStateMachineChanged comment. Do NOT apply a blanket offset when working this class; measure per field. Also confirmed the reference/shims/aiupdatelayout tree is inert - it is not wired into any include path in tools/build.py. One pin added: setEffectivelyDead@Object at ILT thunk 0x00030887 to matched body 0x001C7E60
