external-linkage-emits-without-callers	0x00869E30	7	converted	CORRECTION to a rule I stated too broadly. After two candidates failed with add_match reporting symbol not found in object, I recorded that a function nothing calls cannot be landed because no TU emits it. That is only true for functions whose emission DEPENDS on use - header-inline definitions emitted as COMDATs, and static/internal-linkage functions, both of which the compiler discards when unreferenced. It is NOT true in general. _ArrayGetDataPtr in darray.c has ZERO callers anywhere in Code/, yet it has external non-static linkage, so MSVC emits it regardless, and it byte-verified and landed cleanly. It even carried a stale comment from an earlier agent asserting it was unlandable for want of a caller - that comment was left in place per append-only discipline but is wrong. The corrected rule: check LINKAGE, not caller count. External non-static definitions in a .c or .cpp file are emitted whether or not anything references them; only inline/COMDAT and static definitions need a caller to exist. The two earlier failures (ParticleEmitterClass::Is_Invisible and AIPlayer::isUnderConstruction) are both the inline/header case, so they remain correctly blocked, but for the right reason now
