source-location-is-the-real-blocker		318	identified	THIS CORRECTS MY OWN FRAMING OF THE REMAINING POOL, and it is the most important result of the run. I have been describing the leftover gen-dump rows as LAYOUT-BLOCKED and dispatched a run specifically to split them into classes A (missing prerequisite), B (layout drift), C (named residual), D (wrong pin) and E (structural). THAT TAXONOMY TURNS OUT TO BE THE WRONG QUESTION. Of the 422 one-real-pin candidates, removing the known-blocked families (NetPacket ~49, DX8Wrapper, GeometryInfo, ArchiveFile, the refuted-RVA list) leaves 318 workable. ONLY 32 OF THOSE 318 BELONG TO A CLASS THAT HAS EVEN ONE MATCHED SIBLING ANYWHERE IN functions.csv. The other 286 - ninety percent - have no matched sibling at all, which means there is no evidence in the ledger for which .cpp should define them, and explain_mismatch cannot be pointed at anything because there is nothing to point it at. The binding constraint is SOURCE DISCOVERY, not classification. Of the 32 with a guessable source, a dominant-sibling-file heuristic actually located the symbol in only 8 cases against genuine project source (2 more only resolved against the vendored CnC_Generals_Zero_Hour reference tree, a different game, and were excluded as untrustworthy; 7 were my own tooling's false positives where a scope regex mis-read an STL template parameter as a class name; 15 found nothing after trying up to 8 alternate sibling files). A sub-30-percent hit rate means the heuristic itself is not fit for purpose
