free-functions-trade-one-blocker-for-another	0x0078FDE0	8	identified	THE ANSWER TO 'ARE FREE FUNCTIONS AN EASIER CLASS' IS: SAME RATE, COMPLETELY DIFFERENT BLOCKER, and the blocker is the actionable part. The newly-indexed free functions converted 3 of 8 (37.5%), statistically indistinguishable from the member-function lane's one-in-three - but 3 of 3 of the ones actually taken to a compile, with two matching on the FIRST build, because the other five were refuted from the retail disassembly with no build spent. They DO shed the baggage I expected: none of the three landings needed a class layout or a vtable slot, which are the dominant blockers everywhere else. WHAT THEY PICK UP INSTEAD IS MSVC 7.1'S INTERNAL-LINKAGE CUSTOM CALLING CONVENTION. A file-static free function is exactly what the compiler is permitted to give a private register convention to, and it does: drawCheckBoxText at 0x0078FDE0 opens `mov ebx, eax`, taking its first parameter in EAX, and drawStaticTextText at 0x00796BA0 opens `mov edi, eax` - THERE IS NO C++ SPELLING THAT PUTS A PARAMETER IN EAX, so those rows are unmatchable no matter how good the reconstruction. A third, populateLadderListBox, is the callee-side variant: the static it calls at 0x004D7E60 reads arguments from ecx and edx while the caller cleans with `add esp,4` and the callee ends in a plain ret, so caller and callee share a private convention and the 96-byte body cannot be matched independently of the 1202-byte static. MEMBER FUNCTIONS STRUCTURALLY CANNOT HAVE THIS PROBLEM because __thiscall is fixed by the ABI - ecx there is `this`, not a convention choice. So the two classes are complementary, not ranked
custom-convention-prefilter-added		2	tooling	IMPLEMENTED THE PRE-FILTER THAT FALLS OUT OF THE ABOVE, and validating it against known outcomes caught a false positive that would have been worse than the problem. find_source.py now disassembles the first few instructions of a FREE function's retail bytes and refuses the row if a parameter register (eax/ecx/edx) is READ before any [esp+N]/[ebp+N] argument load, reporting CUSTOM-CONVENTION instead of LOCATED. It is a small dataflow rule rather than a pattern match - a register only counts if nothing earlier in the prologue wrote it - so `xor eax,eax` and other write-first idioms do not trip it. IT IS APPLIED ONLY TO FREE FUNCTIONS, deliberately: on a member function ecx is `this`, so the same rule would flag every thiscall body in the corpus. THE FALSE POSITIVE WORTH RECORDING: my first version flagged shutdownCompleteWOLLoginMenu, WHICH HAD JUST LANDED BYTE-EXACT, because its prologue contains `push ecx` - which is MSVC's four-byte stack-allocation idiom (and `push ebx/esi/edi` is callee-save), not a parameter read. Skipping push instructions fixed it. I only found this because I validated the filter against five rows whose outcome was already known - two proven unmatchable and three proven landable - rather than against the two it was designed to catch. A FILTER TESTED ONLY ON ITS INTENDED TARGETS IS NOT TESTED. Final state: flags exactly drawCheckBoxText and drawStaticTextText across the whole 954-row pool, clean on all four landed rows including the thiscall control. Four rows in the pool are now refuted before any compile (these two plus the two return-type signature conflicts)
