batch-ledger-insert-path-built		326	tooling	BUILT THE THING THE LAST RUN'S COST MEASUREMENT ASKED FOR, because the bottleneck had moved from reconstruction to bookkeeping. add_match.py is correct and its per-row cost is fine for a handful of claims, but a family conversion lands 40 to 115 rows and the tool is invoked ONCE PER ROW - each invocation re-reads and re-parses a 161861-row ledger, re-validates against every existing row, and RE-RUNS THE FULL BYTE VERIFICATION OF THE WHOLE TRANSLATION UNIT. For N rows in one source the parse happens N times and the TU is compiled and compared N times. Measured on the 326-row landing: about twenty minutes of shape analysis against forty of add_match, and the cost SCALES THE WRONG WAY because every landing lengthens the ledger and slows the next one. tools/add_match_batch.py does the same work once: lock, read, parse, validate ALL rows, append ALL, verify each DISTINCT SOURCE once, revert everything on any failure. NOTHING IS RELAXED - the same advisory lock held across the whole pass, and every validation add_match performs applied per row: name already claimed, address already claimed, range overlapping a matched row, replace_rva naming exactly one gen-dump scaffold, ICF-owner agreement. PLUS A CHECK A PER-ROW TOOL STRUCTURALLY CANNOT MAKE: rows in the same batch are validated AGAINST EACH OTHER, because a batch can collide with itself - an earlier run recorded exactly that happening. AND IT IS ALL-OR-NOTHING: if any source fails byte verification, every row reverts and every stripped marker is restored, because a partial family landing leaves the ledger asserting claims whose siblings were rejected for a reason that probably applies to them too
