autocrlf-makes-the-worktree-lie-about-terminators		3	tooling	A CONTRADICTION BETWEEN THREE LANES THAT TURNED OUT TO BE A GIT SETTING, AND MY BRIEF WAS THE THING THAT WAS WRONG. I measured reverse/symbols.csv as PURE LF and told every lane to append LF; two lanes reported back that the file is CRLF and appended CRLF, and one followed the brief. Both were right about what they saw. core.autocrlf IS TRUE IN THIS CHECKOUT: the committed blob is pure LF and the WORKING TREE IS CRLF, because git converts on checkout and normalises back on commit. So an LF append and a CRLF append produce THE SAME COMMITTED BYTES, and check_csv's complaint about `mixed line terminators` was a working-tree artefact that disappears at commit. VERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: normalising the worktree file to uniform CRLF produced a staged diff of 149 ADDED AND 0 REMOVED - i.e. only the genuine new pins - proving the terminator change is invisible to the repository. THE RULE FOR A BRIEF IS THEREFORE: DETECT THE WORKING-TREE TERMINATOR, DO NOT ASSUME ONE, AND DO NOT QUOTE THE BLOB'S. Measuring a file with `git show` and then instructing someone about the file on disk is comparing two different things whenever autocrlf is on.
row-count-changed-and-it-was-not-ours		288	tooling	A SCARE WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE THE FIRST TWO CHECKS I WROTE WERE BOTH WRONG, AND THE HABIT THEY CAME FROM IS THE USEFUL PART. check_csv reported functions.csv at 161860 rows where it had read 161861 all session. A conversion is supposed to be COUNT-INVARIANT - every claim retires exactly one gen_asm scaffold - so a missing row is the single most alarming thing this ledger can show, given the standing rule that nothing gets deleted. MY FIRST CHECK ASKED WHICH ADDRESSES EXIST IN HEAD BUT NOT NOW and reported hundreds of `duplicates`, which was nonsense: DUPLICATE RVAs ARE NORMAL HERE because ICF folds several names onto one address, and existence is the wrong question. THE SECOND COMPARED TOTALS AND DISAGREED WITH check_csv BY ONE, because I was counting the header line and it was not. THE CHECK THAT ACTUALLY ANSWERED IT compared (name, rva) pairs WITH MULTIPLICITY: exactly 288 scaffolds lost, exactly 288 claims gained, zero malformed rows, perfectly balanced. The row count then traced cleanly to UPSTREAM - their Radar::createEvent commit took the file from 161861 to 161860 before we rebased onto it. NOTHING OF OURS WAS LOST. THE GENERAL LESSON: when a ledger count moves, compare KEYS WITH MULTIPLICITY against the exact base you rebased onto, and walk the upstream commits, before touching anything.
