the-null-arm-that-refuses-to-fold-identifies-an-inline	0x00150660	5	converted	A TELL FOR RECOGNISING AN INLINED LIBRARY TEMPLATE RATHER THAN HAND-WRITTEN CONTROL FLOW, WHICH IS A DISTINCTION THE PROJECT NEEDS OFTEN AND HAS FEW HANDLES ON. The family is the vendored OVERRIDE<T>::operator-> inlined into `return m_thing->m_field;`. THE DISCRIMINATOR IS WHAT THE NULL ARM COMPILES TO: retail loads the field with the REGISTER form `mov eax,[eax+0x74]`, and EVERY hand-written control flow with the same block layout lets MSVC constant-fold the known-null pointer into the absolute form `mov eax,[0x74]`. ONLY A NULL RETURNED OUT OF AN INLINED FUNCTION SURVIVES THE FOLD, because the optimiser cannot see through the inline boundary to prove the value. SO: A FOLD THAT RETAIL DECLINES TO PERFORM IS EVIDENCE OF AN INLINE BOUNDARY, which is the mirror image of the older rule that a fold RETAIL LACKS AND YOU HAVE means the source named a composite twice. Both are the same underlying instrument - the optimiser's willingness to fold reads out the source's structure.
