all-or-nothing-batching-survived-a-hard-kill		278	tooling	AN UNPLANNED TEST OF add_match_batch's REVERT PATH UNDER THE WORST CONDITIONS, AND IT PASSED. All four lanes were terminated mid-flight by an API session limit - one of them literally between writing its pins and writing its manifest. A partially-applied batch would have been the expensive failure here: ledger rows asserting claims whose sources were never written, or sources claiming rows the ledger never got. NEITHER HAPPENED. The full gate on the surviving tree reported Functions OK 161860/161860 with all 5128 sources byte-verified, pin consistency 0 new and 0 stale, and only the two known upstream DIR32 reds. EVERY ONE OF THE 278 LANDED ROWS WAS SOUND. The reason is structural: add_match_batch validates the whole batch, appends, then byte-verifies each source and reverts EVERYTHING on any failure, so the ledger is only ever advanced by a batch that already verified. WHAT DID NEED CLEANING was outside the ledger entirely - three zero-row probe TUs left inside Code/, which fail the gate's `byte-match at least one function or delete the file` rule, and two files staged-then-modified. Both were cosmetic. THE OPERATIONAL LESSON: after any lane dies, RUN THE FULL GATE FIRST AND LET IT ARBITRATE rather than assuming the tree is poisoned - and check for zero-row sources, because that is the one kind of damage a killed lane reliably leaves behind.
