icf-twins-must-not-be-claimed-for-the-wrong-owner	0x00607F90	4	identified	A TRAP THAT WOULD HAVE PRODUCED A GREEN GATE AND A FALSE OWNER, CAUGHT BY REASONING ABOUT WHAT A TU CAN EMIT. An STLport `deallocate` for a 0x210-wide element is BYTE-IDENTICAL across two different unclaimed sources, so either could have claimed retail's body at 0x00607F90 and passed. IT WOULD HAVE BEEN WRONG. Retail's 0x00607F50/0x00607F90 are an ADJACENT allocate+deallocate PAIR, and A DESTRUCTOR-ONLY TU NEVER EMITS `allocate` - only a whole instantiation does. So the pair belongs to the instantiating TU, and folding the deallocate onto a destructor-only file would have claimed an ICF twin for an owner that cannot have produced it. THE GENERAL RULE: WHEN A BODY IS BYTE-IDENTICAL ACROSS CANDIDATE OWNERS, ICF MEANS THE BYTES CANNOT DECIDE OWNERSHIP - ask instead which candidate the COMPILER WOULD HAVE EMITTED IT FROM, and let an adjacent sibling body that only one candidate could emit break the tie. Same reasoning settled the 0x70-wide pair at 0x00604FE0/0x00605040.
