store-width-alone-decides-merge-versus-duplicate	0x005CBBA0	13	converted	A NEW CODEGEN TELL, VERIFIED BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN ARGUED, AND IT EXPLAINS A WHOLE CLASS OF NEAR-MISSES. In a null-guarded tail, MSVC 7.1 sometimes DUPLICATES the trailing store, the call and the entire epilogue into the null arm, and sometimes emits a `jmp` and MERGES them. THE DECIDING FACTOR IS STORE WIDTH ALONE: when the last store before the tail is `mov [esi],eax` at displacement zero - two bytes - it duplicates; at displacement 4 or more it merges. ONE SOURCE SPELLING PRODUCES BOTH, proven by compiling identical body text at 0x005CBBA0 (duplicated) and 0x005CD3D0 (merged). So a body that looks like it needs a different source shape from its sibling may need only a different member offset. TWO FLOAT TELLS FROM THE SAME RUN, both about parity: after `fucompp / fnstsw ax`, an EQUAL compare leaves 0x40, which is ONE bit and therefore ODD parity, so `test ah,0x44` + `jp` fires on UNEQUAL - a body whose shape invites `== 0.0f` is really `!= 0.0f`, and the `==` spelling inverts both branch bytes. Separately `test ah,1` + `jne` is the NaN-careless form MSVC emits for `>=`, where spelling it `<` gets `test ah,5` + `jp`.
minimise-by-prefix-was-unsound		193	tooling	A BUG IN MY OWN COMMIT TOOLING, CAUGHT BEFORE IT LANDED, AND THE FIX IS TO STOP IDENTIFYING WORK BY NAME. Every landing this session rebuilds functions.csv as HEAD's bytes plus this lane's claims, so that restored scaffolds sit in their original positions and the C3 conversion gate sees a pure conversion rather than a `wave` commit. THE SCRIPT IDENTIFIED A LANE'S CLAIMS BY TU-NAME PREFIX, and this run broke that assumption twice: one lane added two rows to an ALREADY-COMMITTED TU whose name carries a previous lane's prefix, and another wrote a source OUTSIDE Code/GameEngine/Source/Common entirely, into WWVegas/WWMath. Both would have been mistaken for pre-existing rows and left in place WHILE THEIR SCAFFOLDS WERE RESTORED - silently giving two owners to those addresses, which check_csv would then have reported as a duplicate claim. THE FIX IS TO DEFINE A CLAIM STRUCTURALLY: a claim is any row present in the working tree and absent from HEAD. That is prefix-independent and directory-independent and cannot miss a row added to an existing file. It also now REFUSES rather than proceeds if a claimed address is not owned by exactly one gen_asm scaffold at HEAD, or would end up owned twice.
