icf-fold-wearing-someone-elses-name	0x007E88A0	3	identified	THE SHARPEST INSTANCE YET OF WHY A LEDGER NAME IS NOT A FACT ABOUT A BODY. The five bytes at 0x007E88A0 are matched in the ledger as ?valid@W3DVideoBuffer@@UAE_NXZ. IN THIS CONTEXT THEY ARE THE FESL MESSAGE'S ERROR PREDICATE - a five-byte body is so small that ICF folds it across completely unrelated subsystems, and whichever converter arrived first got to put its name on it. The lane PINNED A SECOND NAME ALONGSIDE rather than landing over the existing one, which is correct: both names are true of those bytes and neither owns them. THE GENERAL RULE THIS SUPPORTS, now seen from three directions in two runs: WHEN A BODY IS SMALL ENOUGH TO FOLD, THE NAME ON IT DESCRIBES ONE CALLER'S VIEW, NOT THE FUNCTION. Related and recorded in the same run: an existing TU models this same FESL message object but stops at 0x10 bytes while its own primitives touch +0x2C and +0x30 - the lane's verdict was that it `isn't wrong, just truncated`, and it used a separate class rather than overwriting, which is the right call when the tree's model is INCOMPLETE rather than CONTRADICTED.
