gamespy-peer-sdk-identified-by-its-own-dispatch-table	0x0085C600	98	identified	THE STRONGEST IDENTIFICATION THIS EFFORT HAS PRODUCED, AND THE EVIDENCE IS RETAIL'S OWN DATA RATHER THAN ANY SHAPE ARGUMENT. At .rdata RVA 0x00D30D50 sits callbackFuncs[]: THIRTY-FOUR SIXTEEN-BYTE ROWS of {int type, copy, free, call} in ascending type order 0..33, closed by a {34,NULL,NULL,NULL} terminator. GameSpy's Peer SDK declares piCallbackType with EXACTLY PI_NUM_CALLBACK_TYPES == 34 IN EXACTLY THAT ORDER, and EVERY ONE OF THE 102 POINTERS IN THAT TABLE LANDS ON A BODY INSIDE THE CLUSTER 0x0085C600..0x0085DF10. That also explains why those bodies have no callers and never surfaced through caller-based analysis: THEY ARE ONLY EVER REACHED THROUGH THE TABLE. Layout fell out of the same evidence and cross-checks: piCallbackData is {type, success, callback, callbackParam, params, ID, inCall} because every *Call trampoline reads callback from +8, success from +4, callbackParam from +0xC; CHATChannelMode is eight ints because piRoomModeChangedCopy copies it with `mov ecx,8 / rep movsd`; piConnection has inRoom[] at +0x390 and callbackList at +0x1818. A NOTE THAT MUST NOT BE GENERALISED: the 2004 carrier ships NO chat/ or peer/ module, so this is RECONSTRUCTION FROM THE 2007 SDK, NOT VENDORING - and 101 of 102 bodies were byte-exact on the first compile, which is nothing like the 51-of-440 the same 2007 SDK scores on the qr2/GP/ghttp core. The lane appended a dated paragraph to PROVENANCE.txt saying exactly that.
some-text-has-no-scaffold-at-all	0x0085C650	29	tooling	A LEDGER SHAPE I HAD ASSUMED COULD NOT HAPPEN, AND MY COMMIT TOOLING REFUSED IT UNTIL CORRECTED. Every landing so far has been COUNT-INVARIANT: a claim retires exactly one gen_asm scaffold, so functions.csv keeps its row count and a change in that count is the alarm. TWENTY-NINE OF THIS RUN'S CLAIMS HAVE NO OWNER AT HEAD AT ALL. They are not conversions - they are NEW COVERAGE over .text that no dump row ever described. The reason is the same fact that identified them: THE GameSpy CALLBACK BODIES ARE REACHED ONLY THROUGH A FUNCTION-POINTER TABLE, so no call site ever pointed a dump generator at them and they were never scaffolded. The ledger total therefore RISES legitimately, 161860 to 161889. My minimal-diff rebuild asserted `exactly one gen_asm scaffold owner at HEAD` and would have aborted the commit; it now distinguishes the three cases - retire a scaffold, ADD new coverage, or REFUSE when a real non-scaffold row already owns the address. THE INVARIANT WAS NEVER `the count is constant`, IT WAS `no address ends up with two owners`, and only the second is worth enforcing.
a-five-byte-thunk-must-not-hold-a-real-name	0x000059ED	2	identified	A LEDGER DEFECT OF THE SAME FAMILY AS THE __emit LIFT, FOUND INDEPENDENTLY IN ANOTHER RANGE, AND IT PERMANENTLY BLOCKS THE REAL BODIES. Two 5-byte ILT THUNKS are matched in the ledger under THE REAL FUNCTION NAMES THEY JUMP TO: 0x000059ED holds ?evaluateBezSegmentAtT@BezierSegment@@... and jumps to the actual body at 0x000B69A0; 0x00043A9A holds ??0BezierSegment@@QAE@XZ and jumps to 0x000B6D00. TWO THINGS ARE WRONG AT ONCE. A five-byte jmp has ONE CONCRETE BYTE outside its rel32, so such a row was never evidence of identity in the first place; and because one name maps to one address, THE REAL BODIES CAN NEVER BE NAMED WHILE THE THUNKS HOLD THEIR NAMES. The lane refused to rewrite another lane's rows and landed the bodies under ?dup_<rva> placeholders carrying the true identity in object-symbol=, which is the right conservative move. THE CLEAN FIX IS TO RENAME THE THUNK ROWS TO ?j_<rva> AND RE-POINT THE NAMES AT THE BODIES. GENERAL RULE: A ROW WHOSE BYTES CANNOT DISTINGUISH IT FROM THOUSANDS OF OTHERS MUST NOT CARRY A SPECIFIC NAME - not a 5-byte thunk, not a 1-byte ret, not an __emit recording.
my-locality-metric-measured-the-wrong-thing	0x000AE0A0	5	tooling	A METHODOLOGICAL ERROR IN MY OWN FILE-SELECTION TOOL, CAUGHT BY THE LANE IT MISDIRECTED. I built an internal-call-locality test to confirm that a gen_asm file's rows really are one translation unit, on the sound principle that a real TU's bodies call each other. I THEN MEASURED IT WRONG: I counted call targets from unclaimed bodies that land anywhere inside the file's ADDRESS RANGE - but those ranges are densely populated with ALREADY-CONVERTED rows, so a call into a converted neighbour counted as `local`. That inflates the score for any range with a lot of prior work, REGARDLESS OF WHETHER THE UNCLAIMED ROWS FORM A UNIT. The lane measured what I actually meant: of 90 unclaimed rows in d_000ae0a0.asm, EXACTLY ONE IS CALLED FROM ANOTHER, and the file is at least five unrelated clusters - MapCache, GameAudio, a Dict reader, Bezier, an AudioEventInfo hashtable. My 53% was an artefact. THE FIX IS TO COUNT UNCLAIMED-TO-UNCLAIMED CALLS ONLY. A second error in the same brief: my `identity leads` were extracted from names in the address range without excluding gen_small ICF-ALIAS rows, so I reported a `GameLogic behaviour-module cluster` that was not there.
equal-length-sweeps-are-blind-to-a-relayout	0x001BE160	2	tooling	ADVICE I PASSED ON CONFIDENTLY AND THAT WAS STRUCTURALLY WRONG, PLUS THE TOOL THAT REPLACES IT. To reconvert Object.cpp after BFME's class relayout I recommended sweeping for a compiled COMDAT OF THE SAME LENGTH as each retail row and diffing. THAT CANNOT WORK HERE: BFME's offsets cross the disp8/disp32 boundary, so a 3-byte `mov ecx,[ecx+0x0C]` becomes a 6-byte `mov ecx,[ecx+0x188]` - THE RELAYOUT CHANGES INSTRUCTION LENGTHS, NOT JUST CONSTANTS. Measured: of 135 unclaimed rows, ONLY 13 HAVE AN EQUAL-LENGTH CANDIDATE AT ALL. Replaced with a structural matcher that normalises each instruction to mnemonic plus operand FORM with displacements and immediates erased, so the two spellings above hash identically, then ranks candidates by how few constants must move - EXCLUDING RELATIVE BRANCH TARGETS, which differ by construction between object and retail and otherwise bury the signal. It immediately surfaced 0x001BE160 as Object::getLargestWeaponRange NEEDING EXACTLY ONE CONSTANT CHANGED, 0x1F0 to 0x264, the already-proven m_weaponSet shift. IT ALSO CONFIRMED A REFUSAL: the 18-byte rows at 0x001BF490/4B0 have FIVE shape-identical candidates each, so the lane that withheld them for want of an arity discriminator was right. CAVEAT IN THE TOOL'S OWN DOCSTRING: erasing constants erases distinctions, so a short body matches everything - below about 24 bytes the output is noise.
concurrent-lanes-make-a-mid-write-read-lie		4	tooling	A CONCURRENCY HAZARD ONE STEP WORSE THAN THE ONE ALREADY RECORDED, AND IT PRODUCES A WRONG ANSWER RATHER THAN A FAILURE. It was already known that a GATE result is untrustworthy while another lane writes the ledger - a scoped run can read mid-write and report a FAIL that never existed. THIS IS THE READ SIDE: a lane's own sweep read reverse/functions.csv while add_match_batch was rewriting it and SILENTLY REPORTED 171 UNCLAIMED ROWS WHERE THERE WERE 135, presenting 21 ALREADY-MATCHED rows as fresh exact hits. Nothing errored; the number was simply false, and acting on it would have meant re-converting rows another lane had just landed and then failing at the one-name-one-address check. THE LOCK IN add_match_batch SERIALISES WRITERS AND READERS TAKE NO LOCK, which is fine for the gate - it re-reads - and not fine for analysis. RULE: RE-READ THE LEDGER AND RE-CHECK ANY UNCLAIMED COUNT BEFORE ACTING ON IT, and treat a count that disagrees with a previous reading as the file having moved under you rather than as a discovery.
