md5-is-confirmed-and-its-round-functions-are-not-the-rfc-spelling	0x00810120	2	identified	THE DIGEST AT 0x00810060 AND 0x00810120 IS MD5, CONFIRMED FROM THE TRANSFORM RATHER THAN GUESSED FROM ITS SHAPE. The update alone shows only a byte count, sixteen bytes of chaining state and a 64-byte block flushed when full -- suggestive but not proof, and it was landed saying so. The transform settles it: four round functions, the shift table, the message-index schedule and all sixty-four sine constants are RFC 1321's. METHOD WORTH REUSING FOR ANY UNROLLED PRIMITIVE: read EIGHT rounds out of the bytes by hand -- the first and last of each of the four groups -- confirm index, shift and constant against the standard tables at those eight points, then GENERATE the remaining fifty-six from the tables. That verifies the schedule at both ends of every group instead of extrapolating from the first constant, and it landed 3765 bytes with all sixty-four rounds correct on the first attempt. TWO THINGS A READER CHECKING AGAINST THE RFC WILL NOT FIND. The first two round functions are spelled the short way -- ((y^z)&x)^z and ((x^y)&z)^y where the RFC writes (x&y)|(~x&z) and (x&z)|(y&~z). They are exact equivalents, not approximations. And the message schedule is built BACKWARDS, from the end of the block down to index zero, each word assembled little-endian from four descending byte reads; the loading loop also REUSES the state variables as its counter and accumulator, which is why the /GZ uninitialised-use check fires and the translation unit needs /RTCu.
three-spellings-one-instruction-count-only-bytes-tell	0x00810120	1	tooling	A SHAPE THAT DEFEATS EVERY CHECK EXCEPT THE BYTES, and it cost four attempts. The MD5 loader accumulates with b = ( b << 8 ) + *--p. Three spellings of that statement compile to the SAME NUMBER OF INSTRUCTIONS and differ only in which register ends up holding the result: with the decrement as a separate statement the byte load moves AFTER the shift; with the byte written on the left of the sum the add reverses direction and stores the other register; only the pre-decrement inside the dereference puts the pointer step first, the byte load second, and the shifted accumulator as the addition's destination. Instruction counts match, mnemonics match, operand FORMS match -- so a shape comparison like tools/struct_match.py cannot separate them, and neither can reading the listing casually. THE GENERAL RULE: WHEN A DIFF SHOWS THE SAME MNEMONICS WITH SWAPPED REGISTERS, THE QUESTION IS OPERAND ORDER AND SIDE-EFFECT PLACEMENT, NOT LOGIC. Probing four spellings in a scratch function and diffing them against each other found it in one round trip, which is faster than reasoning about MSVC's evaluation order.
write-commit-messages-through-a-quoted-heredoc	0x00810120	1	note	A PROCESS CORRECTION THAT HAS NOW BITTEN FOUR TIMES, recorded here because a pushed commit message cannot be fixed without a force-push on a shared branch. Building a commit message inside a double-quoted shell string -- python -c "..." -- lets the SHELL parse it before python ever sees it, so a backtick starts command substitution and the quoted fragment is EXECUTED AND DELETED. The message for 0x00810120 lost the very code fragment its paragraph was about, leaving the sentence 'The byte accumulate is .' The fix is structural rather than a rule to remember: write the message with a QUOTED heredoc, python - <<'PY', which the shell passes through untouched. THE STATEMENT THAT WAS LOST, recorded here so the finding survives: the accumulate is b = ( b << 8 ) + *--p, with the decrement inside the dereference.
