?bfmeCheckVON@@,0x00325680,45,residue,"stdcall predicate that copy-constructs the source's string member into a stack temporary, passes it by value to the manager's virtual slot 22 and reports whether the result is non-null; the first parameter is declared and never read, as the ret 8 requires. The by-value argument is built with the push-then-record-esp idiom and the callee cleans it. 43 bytes with the temporary, the copy constructor call and the virtual call all right. Two gaps: MSVC hoists the manager global's load above the constructor call where retail reads it after, and it renders the null test as neg/sbb/neg rather than retail's xor ecx,ecx / test / setne cl / mov al,cl. Naming the result in a local gets the long bool tail and 46 bytes but keeps the hoisted global; comparing against an explicit null pointer changes nothing. Unlike 0x00628800, where the same long tail came from an uncast bool return of a pointer-to-pointer comparison, a pointer-against-zero comparison does not select it."
?bfmeHasVOM@BfmeSetVOM@@,0x00628800,55,note,"Converted byte-exact but lost to a claim race: another contributor landed it as ?bfmeGo1108A@BfmeW1108@@ while the land was running. Recording the finding because it generalises: the tail xor ecx,ecx / test / setne cl / mov al,cl is a bool return of an UNCAST pointer-to-pointer comparison. Declared char, or with any cast, the tail collapses to setne al and the body comes out six bytes short. Same rule as 0x007E89C0."
