?bfmeReleaseVQA@BfmeMgrVQA@@QAEXHH@Z,0x007F7C00,103,residue,"FESL transactor slot release: thiscall(int a, int b), scans 4 slots of stride 0x94 based at this+0x60 for (m_bfme00 != 0 && m_bfme1c == b && m_bfme20 == a), then calls slot 12 on this->m_bfme1c with (a, b, 0), zeroes elem+0x00/04/08/0c(byte)/0x10, calls the 0x008002C0 buffer append with the empty string on elem+0x14, and zeroes elem+0x1c/0x20/0x64(byte). Size is 103/103 and every instruction matches except the induction variable's base: retail carries esi = &elem + 0x1c (lea esi,[ecx+0x7c]; cmp [esi-0x1c],ebx; cmp [esi],edx; cmp [esi+4],edi; lea ecx,[esi-8]) where every source form I tried carries esi = &elem (lea esi,[ecx+0x60]; cmp [esi],ebx; cmp [esi+0x1c],edx). Tried: index-only accesses (right bias 0x7c in the loop, but the found block recomputes with imul esi,esi,0x94 and copies this into edi -> 122 bytes); a pointer local walked with ++e; a pointer local derived per iteration as &m_bfme60[i]; and an int* induction variable pinned at &m_bfme60[0].m_bfme1c with the head reached by (BfmeSlotVQA *)((char *)k - 0x1c). MSVC re-bases the IV to the lowest member touched in all three pointer forms, so the source must make the head fields unprovably part of the same object (a secondary base sub-object at +0x1c is the untried reading). Declaration order (int i before the pointer) and the ++i,++e increment order are already correct and reproduce retail's xor eax,eax / inc eax placement."
?bfmeFlushVQB@BfmeHubVQB@@QAEXH@Z,0x007FAAB0,120,residue,"FESL hub flush: thiscall with one unused int parameter (ret 4), walks 4 entries of stride 0x10 based at this+4 as a compiler induction variable biased to elem+0xc, guard (m_bfme00 != 0 && m_bfme0c != 0 && m_bfme0c->m_bfme08(m_bfme0c)), then if (--m_bfme44 < 1) calls the 0x007EA590 thiscall on this->m_bfme48, then elem->m_bfme00(elem->m_bfme04, elem->m_bfme08, elem->m_bfme0c->m_bfme04), elem->m_bfme0c->m_bfme0c(elem->m_bfme0c) and zeroes all four dwords. 111 of the 120 bytes are byte-identical -- index-based element access reproduces retail's induction bias, countdown counter, register allocation and shared continue target exactly. The only gap is the argument to the 0x007EA590 call: retail emits lea eax,[edi-4]; neg eax; sbb eax,eax; and eax,edi; push eax, i.e. the branchless select ((char *)this - 4) != 0 ? this : 0, which is a derived-to-base upcast of a pointer valued this-4 onto a base at +4 (so the hub is a secondary base of an owner whose first base is 4 bytes). Modelling that hierarchy (class BfmeOwnVQB : public BfmeHeadVQB, public BfmeHubVQB with the argument static_cast<BfmeOwnVQB *>(this), and the same via an explicit (char *)this - 4 cast and via a named local) makes MSVC pick the BRANCHY adjust instead: lea eax,[edi-4]; cmp eax,ebx; je +5; add eax,4 -> 125 bytes. /G7 gives 123, /O1 gives 108, /O2 /G7 gives 123. The branchless form needs whatever makes MSVC fold the +4 back into the live this register; an integer-typed select ((int)this - 4 != 0 ? (int)this : 0) is the untried reading, cf. sbb-select-needs-inverted-test."
?bfmeFlushVQB@BfmeHubVQB@@QAEXH@Z,0x007FAAB0,120,residue,"Follow-up to the earlier 0x007FAAB0 entry: the sink-call argument is solved. Casting the branchless select straight to the parameter type -- m_bfme48->bfmeDropVQB((BfmeHubVQB *)((int)this - 4 != 0 ? (int)this : 0)) -- gives retail's lea eax,[edi-4]; neg eax; sbb eax,eax; and eax,edi; push eax exactly, and also fixes the receiver-load ordering. Modelling it as a real base cast is what forced the branchy adjust; casting to an intermediate derived type adds a second je/add eax,4/jmp/xor adjustment on top. Body is now 120 of 120 bytes with every instruction and every displacement identical. The only residue is a three-way scratch-register rotation across seven instructions of the three-argument call: retail loads the object into eax (mov eax,[esi]; mov ecx,[eax+4]; mov edx,[esi-4]; mov eax,[esi-8]; push ecx; push edx; push eax) where the source gives ecx (mov ecx,[esi]; mov edx,[ecx+4]; mov eax,[esi-4]; mov ecx,[esi-8]; push edx; push eax; push ecx) -- same instructions, same lengths, first temp in ecx instead of eax and the rest following. Tried and rejected: hoisting the object into a local for the third argument only (identical output), using one local for the third argument and the release call (spills, 131 bytes), a volatile object-pointer member (adds a guard load, 124 bytes), /G7 (118), /Gy (120, same rotation), /O2 /Oy- (133), /Ob1 (120, same rotation)."
