?init@ModuleFactory@@UAEXXZ	0x0012C2E0	18040	mapped	t=175min model=opus5 PHANTOM MODULE AUDIT — the method for telling a BFME module class from a borrowed Zero Hour name, and the two oracles that decide it. (1) THE REGISTRY. BFME names every module it owns by a string literal, from exactly two functions: this one (246 modules) and the W3D draw registrar at 0x006BFFE0 (16 more; today ?d_006bffe0 in Code/gen_asm/d_006b9500.asm). Each registration fills a ModuleTemplate {+0 newModuleInstance, +4 newModuleData, +8 moduleData, +0xC interfaceMask} in one of two compiled shapes — a call to ?addModuleInternal@ModuleFactory@@ (146 sites, args inst/data/0/moduleType/&name/mask) or those same stores inlined after ??AModuleTemplateMap@ModuleFactory@@ (101 sites). tools/module_registry.py reads both into reverse/module_registry.tsv. The 262 names map to 262 DISTINCT newModuleInstance bodies, so the table is a bijection: any ?friend_newModuleInstance@X row on an address it assigns to Y is provably misnamed. 449 ledger rows agree with it, 124 disagree (module_registry.py --check). (2) THE ABSENCE PROOF. The registration macro stringifies the class identifier, so a shipped module class necessarily puts its own name into the image as ASCII; for 100 of Zero Hour's 224 registered module classes the byte sequence occurs NOWHERE in lotrbfme.exe, in any section, as literal or substring. BFME did not ship them, so the 93 ledger rows naming 74 of them are borrowed names. tools/phantom_modules.py -> reverse/phantom_modules.tsv. (3) THE INDEPENDENT CHECK that makes the registry trustworthy rather than merely plausible: every ?friend_newModuleData body ends in INI::initFromINIMultiProc(data, X::buildFieldParse), and that parse proc carries its own class name as a literal. On 0x00114A40/B50/C60/D70 it reads RampageBehavior/EnragedBehavior/HitReactionBehavior/ClickReactionBehavior — agreeing with the registry and contradicting the ledger on all four, a clean one-slot shift. TWO TRAPS. Do not read the module names as one pooled blob: LockWeaponCreate sits at 0x00C8FD94 among thirty neighbours but ModelConditionSoundSelectorClientBehavior is at 0x00C8E57C and W3DTreeDraw at 0x00D1D234, so a windowed scan calls both absent — search the whole file. And do not read absent-from-the-registry as absent-from-the-image: RailedTransportDockUpdate and W3DPoliceCarDraw are each compiled into BFME with their own getModuleNameKey and pool glue referencing their own literal, and neither init registers them. LANDED: 52 duplicate phantom rows retracted from ModuleFactory.cpp (330/330 -> 278/278, addresses claimed by 2+ names 999 -> 987). REMAINING: 42 phantom rows, 31 in ModuleFactory.cpp, 8 in W3DModuleFactory.cpp, 2 in single-row thunk TUs that the source-claims gate would reject if emptied; 38 of the 42 have a registry-proven owner that has no row of its own on those bytes, so each needs a repoint rather than a retraction.
